Done. The RecoveryHintTool wrapper is now live in production. Here's what happened:

What I did:
• Added with_recovery_hints and `ToolFailureTracker to the imports in src/tools.rs`
• Created a single shared ToolFailureTracker instance in `build_tools() — its Arc<Mutex<HashMap>>` interior means all tools share failure counts safely
• Wrapped all 7 file/shell tools (bash, read_file, write_file, edit_file, list_files, search, `rename_symbol) with with_recovery_hints` as the outermost wrapper layer
• Intentionally skipped ask_user, todo, and sub_agent — interactive/meta tools don't benefit from recovery hints
• Removed stale #[allow(dead_code)] annotations since the code is no longer dead

Wrapping order (inside out): guard → confirm → auto_check → truncation → recovery_hints → hook. Recovery hints see the final truncated error, which is what matters for generating useful advice.

What this means in practice: When the LLM's tool call fails, the error message now includes escalating hints — first a diagnostic suggestion ("check the path exists"), then on repeated failures a concrete alternative ("try write_file instead of edit_file"). This helps the agent self-correct without human intervention, closing the gap on graceful degradation.

